WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.103
This is a building collapsing in Turkey

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:05.505
hours after a series of 
earthquakes and aftershocks

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.073
on February 6th.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.542
First, the bottom floor crumbled.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.846
Then the rest of the building 
came down on top of it

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:15.115
in what's known as 
a "pancake collapse".

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.151
Thousands of buildings 
near the epicenter

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:19.953
in southern Turkey and 
northern Syria

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.188
tumbled straight down in a similar way.

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:25.091
The intensity of the earthquakes alone

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.193
carried the potential for destruction.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:29.896
But it's the pancake collapses

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.031
that have made this

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.533
one of the deadliest disasters in the region.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:37.370
Even in very well designed and executed

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:38.872
building would have suffered.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:41.107
This doesn't rule out that 

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:43.443
the buildings were free of defect.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.578
"How well buildings protected residents

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:48.548
played a significant role in lives saved and lost."

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:50.850
"Clearly a lot of these buildings were not up to standard."

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.820
The death toll continues to climb...

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.456
as many victims are still stuck

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.324
under the weight of the buildings.

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.527
So what is it about these buildings

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:05.532
that has made this earthquake 
even more devastating?

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.170
First, let's look at the larger region

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.005
that's prone to severe earthquakes

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.140
because of these two fault lines.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.976
They sit along tectonic plates 
that meet one another

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.211
and cause friction.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:19.446
When the friction builds up...

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:21.781
a powerful earthquake is eventually released.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.917
Over the last century,

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.986
Turkey has seen major earthquakes 
along both fault lines.

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:29.722
The most recent one happened here.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.325
This is the seismic impact 
that shows the epicenter.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.962
When you take a closer look at the most impacted areas...

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.932
they were densely populated with Syrian refugees

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:42.302
who ended up living in buildings that were haphazardly built

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:44.504
and largely neglected.

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:47.807
And across the border in Syria, buildings in these areas

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:50.477
were already weakened by the prolonged civil war.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:54.214
But what really complicates this disaster

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:56.116
is that Turkey was aware of the buildings

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:57.784
that were the most high risk.

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.720
They're called soft story structures.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.623
A soft story building is

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.493
typically a large structure with multiple floors...

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:08.828
and an open plan on the bottom.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.831
That bottom floor can sometimes be a parking garage

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.834
space for small businesses 
or extra homes.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.170
These buildings are very common
throughout the world

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.039
especially in countries like India
Pakistan, and Turkey.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:21.608
Because they offer a solution

00:02:21.608 --> 00:02:24.043
to overcrowding in 
densely populated areas.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.913
But they historically do a poor job

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:29.649
of withstanding medium 
to powerful earthquakes.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:33.720
Here's why: these columns here 
could be made of brittle concrete

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.088
and the stories on top

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.524
might be made of heavier materials like concrete.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.293
Sometimes the bottom floor has fewer walls

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:41.528
than the ones above

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:43.429
with sides that may be left open

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.798
or columns that aren't connected 
by walls.

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:49.402
This means that a soft story is the weakest floor

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:52.138
and cannot fully support the heavier ones above it.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:55.108
So when an earthquake hits the structure may shake.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.477
Until that bottom floor collapses.

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.813
During powerful earthquakes like Turkey and Syria's

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.882
the remainder of the building may follow.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:04.651
This kind of collapse

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.420
traps people under heavy construction materials

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.023
making rescue missions even more difficult.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.572
But this is a challenge Turkey has faced before.

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:30.009
A comparable 7.6 earthquake hit İzmit in 1999

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:32.545
and caused over 17,000 deaths.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:35.848
It became clear that poor building design 
and soft stories

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:38.618
which made up nearly 
90% of building collapses

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:41.087
exacerbated the death toll 
in this disaster.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:44.290
The aftermath pushed the Turkish government 
to reintroduce

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.926
building codes with an emphasis 
on earthquake safety.

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:49.762
But they weren't enforced due to corruption.

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:53.666
And many buildings in Turkey were built too well before 1999,

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:54.968
which meant those buildings

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.270
could only be strengthened retroactively.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:01.674
There is a way to keep 
soft story buildings intact

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.276
in the event of an earthquake:

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.144
retrofitting them with materials

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.146
that can support the weight of the structure.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.915
In an ideal scenario

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.551
these columns can be replaced or reinforced

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:12.919
with steel frames.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:15.421
These open walls can be reinforced too

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:18.759
and extra bolts and braces can be 
drilled into the foundation.

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.526
So if the structure shakes

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.462
there's support from that bottom floor.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.031
A solution like this seem simple

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:27.433
but it's extremely expensive to execute.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.335
According to the World Bank

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:32.572
around 6.7 million residential buildings in Turkey

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:34.574
need retrofitting or reconstruction

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.644
at a cost of $465 billion.

00:04:37.644 --> 00:04:41.581
As of 2021, only about 4% of those buildings 

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:42.448
were transformed.

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:44.851
In fact, retrofitting is an expensive undertaking

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:48.988
and nearly impossible, even 
in countries like the US.

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:51.658
Here is every remaining soft story building in San Francisco

00:04:51.658 --> 00:04:54.060
that needs reinforcements that would cost billions.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.829
But another problem in Turkey 
is that construction companies

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.732
have been cutting corners and 
ignoring building codes

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:00.566
for decades.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.801
And the Turkish government

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.836
has let their violations slide.

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:05.338
According to the BBC

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.607
this post advertised a building that complied

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:10.109
with Turkey's latest earthquake safety regulations

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:12.645
which emphasizes the use of strong materials.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:15.581
But the way that same building collapsed 

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:18.418
suggests it was a soft story structure.

00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:21.054
Now, Turkey is cracking down on contractors

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:25.325
that are allegedly responsible 
for these collapses.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:29.329
The scale of this disaster is a result of complex factors...

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:31.264
like geography

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:33.499
neighborhoods still reeling from war 

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:34.834
and weak buildings.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:38.204
But what makes this moment particularly harrowing

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:41.174
is that while earthquakes along fault lines are inevitable...

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:44.444
many of these deaths didn't have to be.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:46.913
Turkey, along with other countries near fault lines

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:49.382
are all prone to intense earthquakes.

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:51.551
But they won't have to be this deadly

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.353
if cities enforce codes 

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:54.854
and treat safe housing...

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.790
as a human right.

